{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791295595,"nonce":"K/PkKnXbc+F85ZzHn9rVYVNpgJk=","title":"LLVM: late OSR helpers recompile an already-installed normal entry","message":"ZyntaxLlvmBackend::resume_points compiles a complete normal entry plus the requested continuation, although the entry remains installed already. This repeats lowering, optimization, code generation, and retention of unused normal code. Emit only the continuation where stable cross-tier cells exist; recursive calls must use the installed cell, and the existing entry pointer must survive. Keep the existing path for outlined regions (shared entry/helper body), address-taken functions, and backends without cross-tier cells.\n\nExperiment: publishing continuations before normal entry promotion passes correctness tests but regresses NUC Lua tokenize from 166.467 to 223.035 ms kernel and 208.425 to 273.177 ms wall (five alternating checked runs). Traces show the candidate enters the LLVM continuation during the first loop, whereas the baseline finishes that loop before publication. Disabling OSR diagnostically gives 149.802 versus 150.101 ms. Mandelbrot remains about 45 ms; even its separate continuation arrives too late for a useful transfer. Do not enable blanket helper-first promotion. Keep the narrower late-resume-only improvement; follow-up must address call-heavy continuation quality and effective loop specialization.","files":null}]}